  "content": {
    "title": "2026 Masters: How did the honorary starters traditions begin? Go back to 1941",
    "text": "Updated April 3, 2026, 5:08 p.m. ETThe tradition of honorary starters at the Masters unofficially began in 1941 with amateur Francis Ouimet.Craig Wood, previously known as \"The Great Runner-Up,\" won the 1941 Masters after a final-round battle with Byron Nelson.The formal honorary starter tradition was established in 1963 with Jock Hutchison and Freddie McLeod.Eleven former Masters participants, including Jack Nicklaus and Gary Player, have served as honorary starters over the years.Eleven former Masters Tournament participants have served as honorary starters.And while the Thursday tradition formally began in 1963 with Jock Hutchison and Freddie McLeod, it unofficially started in 1941 — 85 years ago — with Francis Ouimet.Ouimet, the revered amateur who won the 1913 U.S. Open over British stalwarts Harry Vardon and Ted Ray at The Country Club in Brookline, Mass., had been invited by Bobby Jones to compete in every Masters since its inception in 1934. Yet each spring, Ouimet politely declined until changing his mind in 1941. Ouimet arrived in Augusta on the eve of the Masters and learned he’d be paired with 11-time major winner Walter Hagen. The tournament would begin at 1 p.m., with Ouimet and Hagen as the opening pairing.Ouimet declared prior to arriving that he would only play 18 holes, and stuck to his decision after shooting 42-40–82. Ouimet competed with borrowed clubs, as his golf bag and luggage were placed on the wrong airplane when he left Boston.“My first visit here, and why, I can’t possibly understand,” said Ouimet, 47 years old at the time, during his only visit to Augusta.It had been planned for Ouimet to attend the awards ceremony and present the winner’s check, but the amateur returned to New England before the final round. Ouimet left a note for Jones’ father, Col. Robert Jones Sr., calling Augusta National, “The finest test I have yet seen or expect to see.”Ouimet earned the pre-tournament headlines, but Craig Wood left Augusta as its newest champion. Wood, of New York, was known as “The Great Runner-Up,” for having placed second at the 1933 British Open, the 1934 PGA Championship, the 1939 U.S. Open and the 1934 and 1935 Masters. Wood entered Sunday with a five-stroke lead over Byron Nelson, but the 1937 Masters winner shot 33 to Wood’s 38 and the tournament was tied with nine holes remaining. Wood, playing 20 minutes ahead of Byron, was on No. 11 when he discovered that Nelson had pulled even. Wood later said, “I knew I had to play my best to win.” Wood birdied the 12th and 13th, while Nelson played them two above par. It marked a rare lowlight in Nelson’s young career, as the same holes had propelled him to victory four years prior. For the week at No. 13, Nelson posted four, five, six, six, while Wood secured four birdies.Ohio Gov. James M. Cox replaced Ouimet as the director of ceremonies. Cox, a member of Augusta National, referenced the raging war overseas, and spoke of the gift of hosting a golf tournament without fearing a bomb strike. He then turned his praise to the former Great Runner-Up.“When I saw Craig Wood take out a 3 iron,” Cox said. “And play that great shot to the 13th green, I said to Grantland Rice, ‘It is Austerlitz. Napoleon has ordered the attack.’”Who have been Masters honorary starters over the years?Jock Hutchison, 1963-73Fred McLeod, 1963-76Gene Sarazen, 1981-99Byron Nelson, 1981-82 and 1984-2001Ken Venturi, 1983Sam Snead, 1984-2002Arnold Palmer, 2007-2015Jack Nicklaus, 2010-presentGary Player, 2012-presentLee Elder, 2021Tom Watson, 2022-present",
